  "summary": "Three schools and a recreation center on one Philadelphia block, set in a landscape the whole neighborhood can use.",
  "cover": "assets/images/garden-schools/aerial-day.jpg",
  "galleryLead": "Views",
  "body": [
    "School buildings usually read as institutions: one featured architectural move, a strong contrast with their surroundings, and above all a secure perimeter. This project starts from what might replace that.",
    "The model borrowed here is the garden cemetery, the first designed public landscape in America, where people walked and picnicked before public parks existed. A garden school works the same way. The grounds are the neighborhood's garden, and the school sits within them rather than behind a fence.",
    "One Philadelphia block holds a Pre-K and kindergarten, an elementary school, and a middle school combined with a library and recreation center, with parking below and a community hub at the eastern end. The landscape between them stays open — wetlands and boardwalk, a learning canopy, theater, pool, fields and courts — so the block remains in use after the school day ends."
  ],
